import{_ as e}from"./chunks/tasks.pSw3Tm7x.js";import{_ as a,o,c as n,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.CS-cBh1g.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Tasks","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"suite/tasks.md","filePath":"suite/tasks.md"}'),r={name:"suite/tasks.md"};function h(d,t,i,c,g,l){return o(),n("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[s('<h1 id="tasks" tabindex="-1">Tasks <a class="header-anchor" href="#tasks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tasks&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Feedback from the team becomes tasks for coding agents. You approve an agent&#39;s plan before it changes code, and check the change when it&#39;s done. Tasks live as one JSON file each in the robot repo&#39;s <code>tools/mayhem/review/</code> folder, so agents and teammates see them with the code. Choose the robot code folder first.</p><p><img src="'+e+'" alt="The Tasks board"></p><h2 id="giving-feedback" tabindex="-1">Giving feedback <a class="header-anchor" href="#giving-feedback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Giving feedback&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Add feedback</strong>: thumbs up, thumbs down or a comment, with a short title and what should change.</li><li><strong>Pit note</strong>: the one-line box at the top. Type and press Save.</li><li><strong>Driver form</strong>: a quick post-match form (ratings, yes/no questions, what broke, what to change), attached to that match&#39;s log.</li><li><strong>Right-click → Make this an agent task</strong> almost anywhere in the app: an auto step, a state, a log moment, a Changes finding, a spot in a sim replay. The task records exactly what you pointed at.</li></ul><p>Add your name so the agent and team know who to ask. In the browser, feedback downloads as a file to put in <code>tools/mayhem/review/</code>.</p><h2 id="the-board" tabindex="-1">The board <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-board" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The board&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Meaning</th><th>You</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>New</strong></td><td>Waiting for an agent.</td><td>Can decline.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Agent planning</strong></td><td>An agent took it.</td><td><strong>Back to New</strong> takes it back if the agent stopped.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Plan to approve</strong></td><td>The agent wrote a plan.</td><td><strong>Approve plan</strong>, or write what should be different and <strong>Ask for a different plan</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Agent working</strong></td><td>Plan approved, code changing.</td><td><strong>Back to New</strong> takes it back if the agent stopped.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ready to check</strong></td><td>The change is done.</td><td><strong>See what changed on the robot</strong> opens Changes for it; then <strong>Accept the change</strong>, or write what&#39;s wrong and <strong>Not right, send back</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Done</strong></td><td>Accepted, declined or just noted.</td><td>Can reopen.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Sending a plan or a change back needs a note: it is what the agent reads to fix it.</p><p>The Tasks tab badge counts plans and changes waiting for you, and the Overview lists them. Each card shows its kind, author, what it&#39;s pinned to, the agent, and the risk level of its latest change. Open a card for the full text, the plan, each change with its findings, and the history.</p><p>The line above the board names the agents on the queue and when each last did something. A task an agent has held for a day without an update is marked <strong>no update for …</strong>; take it back so another agent can pick it up.</p><h3 id="what-gets-accepted-without-you" tabindex="-1">What gets accepted without you <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-gets-accepted-without-you" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What gets accepted without you&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Only two kinds of change skip <strong>Ready to check</strong>: one that changes only auto paths, every one of which still finishes in the preview, and one that touches nothing that reaches the robot (notes, a new scenario). Everything else waits for you, including any change to code Mayhem can&#39;t read. Those show as <strong>Other robot code changed</strong> with the file names; check them with the <a href="/Mayhem/suite/changes#sim-proof">sim proof</a>.</p><h3 id="the-sim-proof-on-a-change" tabindex="-1">The sim proof on a change <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-sim-proof-on-a-change" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The sim proof on a change&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A card in <strong>Ready to check</strong> says whether a sim proof was run on that exact commit: <strong>sim proof passed</strong>, <strong>differs</strong>, <strong>failed</strong>, or <strong>no sim proof</strong>. Open the card to see the run: how many scenarios, which ones failed or behave differently, who ran it and when. That line is written by whatever ran the simulation (the agent&#39;s tool, or this app), never typed in by an agent.</p><ul><li><strong>Run sim proof</strong> (when there is none) opens Changes on the task&#39;s versions and starts it. When it finishes, the result is kept on the task.</li><li><strong>Watch them</strong> opens the same comparison so you can replay the scenarios that changed.</li></ul><p>A newer commit on the same task has no proof until one is run on it.</p><h3 id="after-you-accept" tabindex="-1">After you accept <a class="header-anchor" href="#after-you-accept" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;After you accept&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Accepting is not the end. A done task shows <strong>Where it is now</strong> as four steps, each read from git, the robot and the logs:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Reached when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Accepted</strong></td><td>You accepted the change.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>In main</strong></td><td>Its pull request is merged (a squash merge counts).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>On the robot</strong></td><td>The code on the robot includes it, as of the last <strong>Check robot</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>In a log</strong></td><td>A collected log was recorded on code that includes it. The step names the log and its red flags; <strong>Open log</strong> opens it.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The card&#39;s chip shows the furthest step, and the Overview says how many accepted changes are not merged or not on the robot yet.</p><h2 id="connect-an-agent" tabindex="-1">Connect an agent <a class="header-anchor" href="#connect-an-agent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Connect an agent&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Agents run in a terminal, and that is where you talk to them: an agent that needs to know something asks you there and waits. The app is for feedback, approving plans and checking changes.</p><p>Expand <strong>Connect an agent</strong> at the bottom of the page:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Panel</th><th>What&#39;s there</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Put an agent on the queue</strong></td><td>A copyable prompt, with this robot&#39;s folder filled in, that tells any coding agent how to work the queue. Open the agent in a terminal in the robot code folder and paste it. For Claude Code, the same steps are a skill: copy <code>integrations/skills/mayhem-tasks</code> into the robot repo&#39;s <code>.claude/skills/</code> and run <code>/loop 10m /mayhem-tasks</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Share this project with an agent</strong></td><td>A copyable brief describing the project.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Live file link</strong></td><td><strong>Save project file</strong> links the project to a file in the robot repo; see <a href="/Mayhem/suite/projects#live-file-link-desktop">projects</a>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Connect over MCP</strong></td><td>Setup for the Mayhem MCP server.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Command line</strong></td><td><code>scripts/mayhem.mjs</code> commands.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>C++ library</strong></td><td>Export the routines as a C++ header for the robot.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Project document</strong></td><td>The raw project JSON.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Agents work tasks with the MCP tools <code>list_tasks</code>, <code>claim_task</code>, <code>propose_plan</code>, <code>report_progress</code> and <code>finish_task</code>, and can analyze logs, run scenarios and diff behavior with the others. See <a href="/Mayhem/agents/">Connect an agent</a> and <a href="/Mayhem/agents/tasks">How agents work tasks</a>.</p><p>After an agent edits a routine, look at the field and graph, then preview it before running it on the robot.</p>',27)])])}const u=a(r,[["render",h]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
